course: technology
lesson: 38
title: Satellites and GPS
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A satellite does not float in space; it is falling all the time. It was thrown sideways so fast by a rocket that as it falls, the round Earth curves away beneath it, so it keeps falling around the Earth and never hits the ground, like a ball thrown so hard it would go right round the world.
explain: Thousands of satellites are up there doing jobs. Some photograph clouds for the weather forecast, some beam TV to the dishes on your street, some carry internet to farms and ships, and some help your phone know where it is.
explain: GPS works like a game of listening. Each GPS satellite keeps perfect time with an atomic clock and keeps calling out the time. Your phone hears several of them, works out how long each call took to arrive, and from that how far away each satellite is, and so exactly where you are.
explain: Space is getting crowded, too: old satellites and broken pieces race round the Earth as space junk, and even a fleck of paint at that speed can damage a spacecraft.
fact: A satellite is a machine launched into orbit, falling around the Earth fast enough that it never hits the ground; there are thousands of working satellites around Earth.
fact: GPS works because a phone listens for timing signals from several satellites and works out where it is from how long the signals took; GPS satellites carry very precise atomic clocks.
fact: GPS is run by the US Space Force, and Europe runs its own system called Galileo.
fact: A sat nav uses GPS for its position and a stored map to find a route; the driver must still watch the road, because sat navs have sent lorries down tiny lanes.
fact: A satellite TV dish collects weak signals from a satellite far above the equator and focuses them onto a receiver, so in the UK all the dishes on a street face south-east, the same way.
fact: Weather satellites photograph clouds and measure temperatures, which helps forecasters predict storms days ahead.
fact: Satellite internet uses many small satellites in low orbit to pass signals between dishes on the ground, so even remote places can get online; companies like Starlink run thousands of them.
fact: A rocket burns fuel and pushes hot gas downward very hard, which pushes the rocket up into space, and some rockets now land back on Earth to be used again.
fact: The International Space Station is a big laboratory orbiting Earth with astronauts on board, and it goes around the Earth about every 90 minutes; the astronauts float because they and the station are falling around the Earth together, though gravity is still there; you can sometimes see it from the ground as a bright moving dot.
fact: Space junk is broken satellites and pieces orbiting Earth very fast, and space agencies track it because even a small fleck of paint can damage a spacecraft.
fact: A map app combines GPS with maps and live traffic from many phones: when lots of phones on one road move slowly, it shows a jam.
fact: Share your location only with your family.
exercise: Why doesn't a satellite fall down to Earth? | It moves sideways so fast that it keeps falling around the Earth | There is no gravity at all in space | It is held up by a long invisible string | It floats on the air like a balloon
exercise: About how many working satellites are around Earth? | Thousands | About ten | Exactly one | None, they all fell down
exercise: How does a phone use GPS to find where it is? | It times signals from several satellites | It asks the nearest phone mast to look for it | It takes a photo of the street and matches it | It guesses from the weather and the time of day
exercise: What very precise thing does every GPS satellite carry? | An atomic clock | A big telescope | A map of every street | A spare astronaut
exercise: Who runs GPS? | The US Space Force | The British Post Office | Every phone company together | The Moon base
exercise: What is Europe's own satellite navigation system called? | Galileo | Apollo | Hubble | Voyager
exercise: Why must a driver still watch the road with a sat nav? | Sat navs can send cars and lorries the wrong way | Sat navs drive the car by themselves once you've typed in where you want to go | Sat navs switch off in towns | Watching the road charges the sat nav
exercise: Why do all the satellite dishes on a UK street face the same way? | They point at satellites high above the equator to the south-east | They are turned to face the wind | They all point at the nearest phone mast | They follow the Sun across the sky
exercise: How do weather satellites help forecasters? | They photograph clouds and measure temperatures | They push the rain clouds away from big cities so they stay dry | They make rain fall on farms | They measure the depth of the sea
exercise: How can you get internet in the middle of nowhere? | Satellite internet from many small satellites in low orbit | A very long extension cable | Shouting to the nearest town | Waiting for a pylon to pass
exercise: How does a rocket get into space? | It pushes hot gas down hard, which pushes it up | It is pulled up by the Moon's gravity once it's high enough | It climbs a very tall tower | It floats up like a hot-air balloon
exercise: What is new about some of today's rockets? | They land back on Earth to be used again | They can fly all the way up without any fuel | They are made of light wood so they weigh less | They carry their own pylons with them into space
exercise: How long does the International Space Station take to go round the Earth? | About 90 minutes | About a whole day | About a week and a half | About a year, like the Earth
exercise: What is space junk? | Old satellites and broken pieces orbiting Earth | Rubbish that the astronauts throw out of the window on purpose every day | Rocks that fall from the Moon | Shooting stars burning up
exercise: How does a map app know there is a traffic jam? | Many phones on one road are moving slowly | A helicopter watches every single road all day | The police send a text to the app for each jam | It guesses from the time of day and the weather
exercise: Who should you share your location with? | Only your family | Anyone who asks online | All your game friends | Strangers who seem nice
test: «astronauts float on the space station cos there's no gravity up there, easy» What is the best correction? | Gravity is still there; they float because they and the station are falling around the Earth together | Right, gravity stops above the clouds | They wear magnetic boots that lift them | The station is filled with helium
test: In a cartoon, a satellite crashes because its engine runs out of fuel. What keeps a real satellite up? | Its sideways speed makes it keep falling around the Earth | Its engine must run all the time, day and night, or it drops straight down | Air under it holds it up | Strings to the ground hold it
test: «sat nav says turn left into the river, it must be right» What is the right answer? | The driver must watch the road and not follow a mistake | Sat navs are never wrong, so turn | Drive in slowly to check | Switch the sat nav off and on again, and if it still says left, then turn left
test: What would happen to the satellite TV if a big tree grew between a dish and the south-east sky? | The picture could break up or stop, because the signal is blocked | The TV would get clearer pictures | Nothing, signals go through anything | The dish would turn round by itself
test: «that bright dot moving fast across the sky, is it a UFO» What is the likely answer? | It may well be the space station, seen from the ground | It is surely a spaceship from Mars coming to visit the Earth | It is just a star, as stars always move that fast | It is a satellite falling to Earth
test: Count them: the space station goes round the Earth about every 90 minutes. About how many times does it go round in 3 hours? | 2 | 3 | 1 | 90
test: Count them: a phone hears 4 GPS satellites, and later 3 more come into view. How many does it hear now? | 7 | 4 | 3 | 12
test: Count them: a street has 5 houses, and every second house has a dish, starting from the first. How many dishes? | 3 | 2 | 5 | 4
test: «stranger in my game asked where i live, should i send my location» What is the rule? | Never; share your location only with your family | Send it if they are the same level as you in the game | Send it if they promise to send theirs to you first | Send only the street name and the town, not the house number
test: Why can a tiny fleck of paint damage a spacecraft? | In orbit it moves extremely fast | Space paint is made of metal | It is red-hot from the Sun | It explodes when it touches glass
test: Why does a GPS satellite need such a precise clock? | The phone works out distance from tiny differences in time | The astronauts need to know the time | The clock keeps the satellite in orbit by ticking at exactly the right speed | The clock powers the satellite
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an artificial satellite does not descend to the surface of the Earth. | Its horizontal velocity is such that it falls continuously around the Earth | Gravity is entirely absent beyond the atmosphere | It is supported by the atmosphere beneath it | It is suspended by cables from the surface
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the method by which a telephone determines its position by GPS? | It measures the time taken for signals from several satellites to arrive | It requests its position from the nearest telephone mast | It photographs the surrounding streets | It estimates its position from the prevailing weather
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the period of revolution of the International Space Station. | It completes one orbit of the Earth in approximately ninety minutes | It completes one orbit in approximately one day | It completes one orbit in approximately one year | It remains stationary above a single point upon the surface of the Earth at all times
exam: Ascertain the means by which a map application detects congestion upon a road. | It observes that numerous telephones upon that road are moving slowly | It receives reports exclusively from a helicopter that patrols the roads continuously | It is notified by text message from the police | It infers congestion solely from the time of day
